          %h2#integration Integration
          %p
            By the discussion in
            %em
              %a{:href => "#DifferentialForms"} Differential forms
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#PrincipalConnections"} Principal connections
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            and more generally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connections
            may be regarded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measures"} measures
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %em change
            = succeed "." do
              %em displacement
            %em
              %a{:href => "#Differentiation"} Differentiation
            showed how to extract from a
            %em finite
            but
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesive
            (e.g. smoothly continuous) displacement all its infinitesimal measures of displacements by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiation"} differentiation
          %p
            Here we discuss the reverse operation:
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            is a construction from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            of the corresponding finite cohesive displacement. More generally this applies to any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            and is then called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
            of the connection, a term again referring to the idea that a finite displacement proceeds pointwise in parallel to a given infinitesimal displacement.
          %p
            Under good conditions this construction can proceed literally by “adding up all the infinitesimal contributions” and therefore
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            is traditionally thought of as a generalization of forming
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sums"} sums
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi Σ
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_{\Sigma} \omega
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            over a space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi S
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{S} f
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum"} sum
            of values of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi Σ
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P} \exp(\int_\Sigma \omega)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            data to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            data (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"}> exponentiated Lie algebra data
            ”).
          %p
            However, both from the point of view of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            as well as from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+abstract"} general abstract
            perspective of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            another characterization of integration is more fundamental: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_\Sigma \omega
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            (or more generally of a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} connection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant"} invariant
            under those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            that are trivial on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+construction"} universal
            such invariant, hence is uniquely characterized by this property.
          %p
            In traditional accounts this fact is referred to via the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stokes+theorem"} Stokes theorem
            and its generalizations (such as the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+Stokes+theorem"} nonabelian Stokes theorem
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
            is indeed
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant"} invariant
            under gauge transformations of differential forms/connections. That this invariance actually characterizes the integral and the parallel transport is rarely highlighted in traditional texts, but it is implicit for instance in the old “path method” of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
